# CODEOWNERS file example

# These owners will be the default owners for everything in the repo.
# Unless a later match takes precedence, @global-owner1 and @global-owner2
# will be requested for review when someone opens a pull request.
# *       @global-owner1 @global-owner2

# Order is important. The last matching pattern has the most precedence.
# So if a file matches multiple patterns, the last matching pattern in the
# file will be the one that takes effect.

# You can specify individual users or teams as code owners.

# Backend code owners
# /backend/               @backend-team

# Frontend code owners
# /frontend/              @frontend-team

# Database specific files
# /database/              @database-team

# CI/CD configuration files
# /.github/workflows/     @devops-team
# /gitlab-ci.yml          @devops-team

# Documentation owners
# /docs/                  @documentation-team

# Specific file owners
# /Dockerfile             @devops-team
# /docker-compose.yml     @devops-team
# /README.md              @documentation-team

# You can also use patterns to match file types
# *.js                    @javascript-team
# *.py                    @python-team
# *.md                    @documentation-team

# When a file matches multiple patterns, the last matching pattern takes precedence.
# For example, if a JavaScript file is in the /frontend/ directory, @frontend-team
# will be its code owner because it is the last matching pattern.
